## Companies That Provide High-Bandwidth Memory (HBM) to NVIDIA

The three main companies that supply high-bandwidth memory (HBM) to NVIDIA — making them critical suppliers for NVIDIA's most high-performance products such as the GB200 Grace Blackwell Superchip (which uses up to ~864GB of HBM3E per rack, with each Blackwell GPU drawing substantial memory for inference-time compute scaling) — are:

### 1. SK Hynix (South Korea) — Primary / Main Supplier
- SK Hynix is NVIDIA's **primary and leading HBM supplier**, holding dominant market share (>50% of HBM3E output).
- Until approximately March 2024, SK Hynix was essentially the sole HBM supplier to NVIDIA.
- It remains NVIDIA's main HBM supplier for the GB200 (Blackwell) platform and prior generations (H100/H200/B200).
- SK Hynix is currently sampling/evaluating TSMC logic nodes for its base die to pursue HBM4, targeting next-gen chips for NVIDIA's Rubin R100.

### 2. Samsung Electronics (South Korea)
- Samsung has been intensifying its bid to break into NVIDIA's HBM supply chain after lagging behind SK Hynix and Micron.
- Samsung has reportedly been working to get its HBM4 chips certified by NVIDIA.
- While it has struggled to catch up in terms of mass production compared to SK Hynix, Samsung is expected to mass-produce next-generation HBM chips in the first half of 2026 and aims to compete more aggressively with SK Hynix and Micron.

### 3. Micron Technology (United States)
- Micron has also made significant progress and has shipped samples of its HBM4 products to customers.
- Micron supplies HBM for NVIDIA's H200 GPUs, indicating it is an established secondary supplier in NVIDIA's HBM portfolio.
- Micron's HBM3E utilizes 1b-nm DRAM chips, putting it on par with SK Hynix's current offerings, and it continues to be an active competitor in this space.

### Why They Are Critical for Products Like the GB200
NVIDIA's GB200 Grace Blackwell Superchip relies heavily on HBM3E memory to feed large AI models efficiently during inference-time compute scaling. Each rack can feature up to ~864GB of HBM3E across multiple Blackwell GPUs, with massive memory bandwidth requirements (up to 16TB/sec per superchip). Because performance is directly tied to data access speeds, these three suppliers form the backbone of NVIDIA's AI hardware supply chain — without their HBM shipments, NVIDIA cannot scale its high-performance AI accelerators.

